.include "8clocks.jsim"
.include "nominal.jsim"
.include "stdcell.jsim"

*******************************************************************************
* Plug-in your full adder and 32-bit ripple-carry adder subcircuits here.
*******************************************************************************
.subckt fa a b cin sum cout

    Xinva a inva inverter
    Xinvb b invb inverter
    Xinvc cin invc inverter

    Xnand1 inva invb cin nand1 nand3
    Xnand2 inva b invc nand2 nand3
    Xnand3 a invb invc nand3 nand3
    Xnand4 a b cin nand4 nand3

    Xsum nand1 nand2 nand3 nand4 sum nand4

    Xnand5 inva b nand5 nand2
    Xnand6 a invb nand6 nand2
    Xnand7 nand5 nand6 sum1 nand2

    Xcarry1 a b carry1 nand2
    Xcarry2 sum1 cin carry2 nand2
    Xcarry3 carry1 carry2 cout nand2

.ends fa

.subckt add32 a[31:0] b[31:0] cin sum[31:0] cout[31:30] 

    X3fa a[31:0] b[31:0] cout[30:0] 0 sum[31:0] cout[31:0] fa

.ends add32

*******************************************************************************
* Your new counter32 subcircuit code should go here.
*******************************************************************************
.subckt counter32 reset a[31:0] f[31:0] clk
  Xadd a[31:0] f[31:0] 0 next_f[31:0] nc[31:30] add32 

  .connect 0 zero[31:0]
  
  Xmux reset#32 next_f[31:0] zero[31:0] mux_out[31:0] mux2
  Xdreg mux_out[31:0] clk#32 f[31:0] dreg
.ends counter32

*******************************************************************************
* Pitfall: DON'T use numbers AND iterators in your node names at the same time,
*          e.g., temp[31:0] and temp2[31:0] will result in conflicts!
*
* Also, you may need to use these statements when making your own counter32:
*
* .connect node othernode(s)...
*  = connects the first node to all the subsequent nodes
*    - For example, ".connect a b[31:0]" connects a to b31, b30, ..., b0.
*    - You can also connect nodes directly to ground ("false") by saying
*      ".connect 0 othernode(s)..."
*    - You can also connect nodes directly to vdd ("true") by saying
*      ".connect vdd othernode(s)..."
*
* Xid s d0 d1 z mux2
*  = "mux2" defines a 2-way multiplexer circuit.
*    - If s = 0, node z is set to the value of node d0.
*    - If s = 1, node z is set to the value of node d1.
*    - s, d0, d1 and z are SINGLE BITS. As usual, use iterators to create
*      several multiplexers at once.
*    - Note that the reset node of your counter32 is only a single bit
*      (NOT 32 bits), so to use it with multiple multiplexers, use the form
*      "node#n" to make n copies of the node, e.g., "reset#32".
*
* Xid d clk q dreg
*  = "dreg" defines a flip-flop register q (a single bit of "memory") and sets
*    it to the value of another node, d.
*    - Unlike "connect"-ing nodes, the value only gets stored into q when clk
*      is doing a rising transition (also known as a "positive edge trigger").
*    - Hint: You will also need iteration for this since we need a 32-bit
*      register, which means you will also need to make 32 copies of clk.
*******************************************************************************

*******************************************************************************
* Finally, it's time to simulate.
* This circuit block instantiates the counter32 circuit that you just wrote.
* We pass it a single-bit reset signal, a 32-bit increment value, a 32-bit
* register, and a clock signal (we just used the JSim-predefined clk1).
*******************************************************************************
Xcounter32 reset incr[31:0] reg[31:0] clk1 counter32

*******************************************************************************
* This "reset" waveform sets up a reset signal that has a delayed start
* and then goes to the next value every 50ns.
* The values "1 0 0 0 1 0" indicate that the reset signal only turns on twice.
* (But at which times?)
*******************************************************************************
Wreset reset nrz(0, 5, 50ns, 14.8ns, 0.001ns, 0.001ns)
+ 1 0 0 0 1 0

*******************************************************************************
* This "incr" waveform sets up a increment value for your counter32 circuit.
* Note that it does NOT have a delayed start. The values "0 1 3 -1" roughly
* translate to the following "commands" in Java-like syntax:
*   reg += 0;  //  0 means don't touch the value
*   reg++;     //  1 means increment by 1
*   reg += 3;  //  3 means increment by 3
*   reg--;     // -1 means decrement by 1
* But note that these "commands" will happen EVERY positive clock edge (and
* only when your reset signal is off)!
*******************************************************************************
Wincr incr[31:0] nrz(0, 5, 50ns, 0ns, 0.001ns, 0.001ns)
+ 0 1 3 -1

*******************************************************************************
* Plot our reset waveform, our incr waveform, clk1, and your 32-bit register.
* Note: sd() displays a range of nodes as a signed decimal (instead of hex)
*******************************************************************************
.tran 300ns
.plot reset
.plot sd(incr[31:0])
.plot clk1
.plot sd(reg[31:0])



*******************************************************************************
* a. Explain the behavior of the 32-bit register output lines, reg(31,0),
* as it is increasing, decrementing and when the output displays an updated value
* In particular, explain the significant delay between when the reset/incr[31:0] values
* change and when the output displays an updated value.
*  
*   Incrementing: When incrementing (incr[31:0] > 0),
*   the adder calculates a larger next value. However, reg[31:0] does not immediately increase. 
*   The new value is loaded into the register only at the next active clock edge.
*   Decrementing: When decrementing (incr[31:0] < 0),
*   Similarly to the incrementing, the adder calculates a smaller next value. However, reg[31:0] does not immediately decrease
*   The new value is loaded into the register only at the next active clock edge.
*   Resetting: When resetting (reset = 1), the register is set to zero. However, reg[31:0] does not immediately reset to zero.
*   The new value is loaded into the register only at the next active clock edge.
*
*   There is a significant delay between when the reset/incr[31:0] values 
*   change and when the output displays an updated value because the register 
*   only updates its value on the rising edge of the clock signal (clk1=1) 
*   and prevents any changes in Dreg while it is in the rising edge of the clock signal.
*   This means that any changes to the input signals (reset or incr) 
*   will not be reflected in the output until the next clock cycle,
*   resulting in a delay between input changes and output updates.
*   Dreg holds the value of from the mux and prevents from having new data or value 
*   added until that value is carried over to the register through the next clock cycle.
*   I believe this is so that it is to prevent data from being lost and overwritten
*   before it is stored in the register.
* 
* b. What is stopping you from making a superfast clock to make a super-fast counter.
*   A super-fast clock cannot be used because the clock period must be strictly greater
*   than the total propagation delay of the circuit's critical path. In this design,
*   the critical path is dictated by the 32-bit ripple-carry adder, where the carry bit 
*   must propogate sequentially through 32 individual full adders before the final sum is valid.
*   Additionally, the signal must pass through the multiplexer and meet the D-register's setup time. 
*   If the clock speed exceeds this total delay, the register will capture invalid, transitioning data on its rising edge.
*******************************************************************************
